{{ echo |> markdown }}
I just noticed… the [Wipe Out](#:~:text=Wipe%20Out) medal image is not a monster devouring people, but a giant wave… which makes sense. _The more you know!_ And damn the [Put 'Em Down](#:~:text=Put%20%27Em%20Down) one is dark, geez…

## Impressive. Very nice. Now let’s see Uncharted 3’s card.

Uncharted 3 came out on the 1st of November 2011 (1/11/11 or 11/1/11 in the U.S., nice — except it was a holiday in France so it came out on 2/11/11, not nice), so I was excited to get this stats card service up and running for the newest Uncharted game! However, while Naughty Dog did have a U3 player stats page, shortly after launch, it changed and it wasn’t possible to scrape it as before. Aside from the DOM being different, they changed the way the pages were served from `GET` to `POST` so I couldn’t just call up the URL, and the web host I used, thought that my server sending a `POST` request to another was fishy and blocked it, causing the service to go dark on the 22nd of November 2011. It was restored the on the 25th of February 2012, thanks to help from somebody I only ever knew as **wx327**, but ultimately the service wasn’t up to the task. Had I had better skills at the time, I probably would  have been able to make it all hold together, but that wasn’t in the cards (if you’ll pardon the pun).
{{ /echo }}

{{ set imageUrl = './generator-u3-nk.avif' |> toRoot }}
{{ image imageUrl, "The generator for Uncharted 3 on Naughty-Kennel.com, a stylised form asking you to provide your username and select a character and background to decorate the image. A total user count of 5456 is displayed.", "This looks half-baked because it kinda was, unfortunately!", { ratio: "1200/753" } }}

{{ echo |> markdown }}
So while I was able to capture _some_ data, it was very limited, and ultimately not viable to create the same level of detail I had for Uncharted 2. The last data capture would occur a whole year before Uncharted 2’s last capture, on the 13th of April 2012, and the data I had captured for my own account was sparse. Despite spending so, so many hours playing Uncharted 3 (especially co-op) with my friends, that data is gone.

Hey, anybody remember the “announcer” telling you your kickback is ready? Fun times.
{{ /echo }}

{{ set imageUrl = [assets.images, 'u3-card-jakhris.png'] |> toPath }}
{{ image imageUrl, "My stats card for Uncharted 3 online, with seemingly little captured data, showing only 16 games played", "That’s barely any data :(", { ratio: "500/125" } }}

{{ echo |> markdown }}
## What about Uncharted 4?

Okay now you’re just asking for trouble! I did play a little U4 with my friends, but barely, which was during my studies, with all my friends also studying. Not to mention, on the PlayStation 4, online gaming was no longer free, so I’ve pretty much dropped off from playing online games since 2014, more or less.

## Online gaming… has changed

It’s a little sad, no longer playing online, given how many amazing memories I made with friends on games such as:
- Warhawk (no [archive](https://warhawk-ps3.github.io/) for me)
- Battlefield: Bad Company 2 ([archived stats!](https://bfbcs.rip/stats_ps3/JaKhris))
- Resistance: Fall of Man and 2 (and a tiny bit of 3?)
- Skate 2 and 3
- Uncharted 2 and 3 (of course)
- Grand Thank Auto IV and V Online

I sank an ungodly amount of hours into online gaming on PlayStation 3 (most stats are lost to time), and some money into DLC, too. Not to mention PAYDAY 2 on PC which occupied all of my evenings during the last year of my studies… when I was “too busy to run a web site”… err… At that point I think I knew I was dropping out, so I spent 150 hours on that game over 6 months, yikes. Anyways, those people are still my best friends today and I’m going to see them next month (I live 7,000 Km away from them… so it’s a rare occasion). Video games can bring ~~young nerds~~ ++people++ together!

## Having a crack at another game

I did help set up a similar stats card service a while back on [Ratchet-Galaxy](https://ratchet-galaxy.com), a Ratchet & Clank fan site I’ve been involved in since 2007. This was when **Ratchet & Clank (Future): A Crack In Time** was out in late 2009, and there was a page Insomniac Games had for PlayStation Network users, listing how many enemies they’d killed, playtime, money collected… things of that nature (pretty unusual offering for a single-player game).
{{ /echo }}

{{ set imageUrl = [assets.images, 'acit-card-jakhris.png'] |> toPath }}
{{ image imageUrl, "My stats card for Ratchet & Clank: A Crack In Time, with various single player stats", "I turned 195 enemies into monkeys!", { ratio: "500/125" } }}

{{ echo |> markdown }}
We had set up the card service, and eventually Insomniac Games got in touch with us, wanting to make sure this was above-board. It was a friendly call, but given we were using imagery from the game, and had some ads running to cover server costs (we made _cents_!), we were kindly asked to remove those, and insert some “unofficial” language here and there — I’ll pretend it was because looked **so dang good** it seemed official. They were (and are) supporters of the fan site, so I believe they even set us up with an API to stop hammering their servers — whoops. You’d think I would have known better… In any case, those stats pages, as well as the stats cards, were eventually retired, and I don’t believe there are data backups. What an era.
{{ /echo }}

{{ set imageUrl = './generator-acit-rg.avif' |> toRoot }}
{{ image imageUrl, "The generator for Ratchet & Clank, a stylised form asking you to provide your username and select a character and background to decorate the image.", "Thanks to the Internet Archive, I can provide a screenshot!", { ratio: "1200/600" } }}

{{ echo |> markdown }}
## A little aside on how I revived this project

I asked on Mastodon how you’d revive a PHP 5 project on a Mac, and [Robb suggested Yerd](https://social.lol/@robb/117231034764829791). The U.I. is clean and straightforward, but it doesn’t support old-ass legacy PHP versions. My project ran on PHP 5.2, and Yerd supports PHP 8, and one major earlier version: PHP 7.4… so after upgrading some various bits of syntax (not all, so there are warnings are all over the place), the website was running well enough to let me look at everything (besides the URL rewriting via `.htaccess` not working, but that’s fine). What a blast from the past… I might just compile a static version of the entire website some day!

Learning lots of PHP over time, it is fun to see how differently I structured everything between JAKPRO and the NK, which were both multi-lingual, multi-theme websites (that’s my signature move), and also quite terrifying to see the code I wrote back then. But it was never compromised, so I must have been doing something right. I did, however, have non-UTF8-encoded backups for JAKPRO, so any accented letter became `�`, which isn’t terribly important but still a silly mistake.

**I’ll end as I started: thanks to Vale for making all the right synapses fire in my brain. This was one hell of a nostalgic trip.**

<hr>

PS: I feel like it’s good to cover my bases here… any and all imagery and iconography taken from the Uncharted games you see in this post belong to Naughty Dog and Sony Computer Entertainment, reproduced here for entertainment purposes in this blog post. I was certainly skirting some fine line of intellectual property law back then, eh?
{{ /echo }}

